'2026-07-01T17:00:00Z'::timestamptz,'news','{"updated":"2026-10-14T19:58:00Z","intro":["New World screwworm was confirmed in Texas livestock in early June 2026, prompting a coordinated response by the U.S. Department of Agriculture and state animal-health officials. The parasite''s larvae can damage living tissue in livestock, wildlife, pets and occasionally people. Confirmed detections in Zavala, La Salle and Andrews counties helped intensify surveillance and control activity near the U.S.–Mexico border.","The original Keep TX Red report at this July 1, 2026 URL was lost from the surviving newsroom records. This October reconstruction uses dated USDA announcements and the Texas Animal Health Commission''s reporting page to explain what officials knew at the time, without implying the July situation was the same as the later October outbreak status."],"sections":[{"heading":"How the first Texas cases were confirmed","paragraphs":["On June 3, 2026, USDA''s Animal and Plant Health Inspection Service announced a laboratory-confirmed New World screwworm detection in a calf in Zavala County. The agency identified larvae in the young calf''s umbilical area and described surveillance, treatment and containment measures. The announcement concerned a confirmed animal case, not simply a warning that the parasite might eventually arrive.","USDA reported additional detections on June 5 and June 8, including a calf in La Salle County and a dog in Andrews County. Investigators were examining links and animal movements, and an early dog-case report noted recent travel to Mexico. Officials published updates as more testing was completed, so the count in any individual June news bulletin reflects a particular date rather than a permanent outbreak total."]},{"heading":"What New World screwworm is","paragraphs":["The species Cochliomyia hominivorax is a parasitic fly whose larvae feed on the living tissue of warm-blooded animals. Unlike many ordinary flies whose larvae consume dead tissue, a New World screwworm infestation can progress rapidly in living wounds. Livestock producers, veterinarians and wildlife managers take these infestations seriously because of animal suffering, risk of severe wounds and economic consequences.","People should not attempt to identify a screwworm infestation merely from a frightening headline or a low-quality photograph. USDA and state animal-health guidance describe appropriate reporting and veterinary procedures. A suspect animal needs professional evaluation, sampling and approved treatment; untreated wounds can worsen while other conditions can look similar."]},{"heading":"The federal and Texas response","paragraphs":["USDA''s June 9 announcement described continued coordination with Texas and New Mexico officials, including the Texas Animal Health Commission, to investigate confirmed detections and intensify surveillance. State and federal teams were conducting field work and tracing possible exposures. This is a disease-control response involving animal-health authorities, not merely a political statement about border security.","Monitoring animal movements, testing suspect cases and communicating with veterinarians can help identify affected areas. Authorities may also adjust movement requirements as conditions change. The official current-status dashboard is therefore more reliable for livestock transport and quarantine decisions than an archival July article, particularly when restrictions have been updated."]},{"heading":"Why sterile insect dispersal matters","paragraphs":["USDA announced in January 2026 that it was adjusting sterile fly dispersal efforts to reinforce a corridor along the U.S.–Mexico border. The control strategy releases sterile male flies so that mating with wild females produces no viable offspring, reducing the potential for the population to reproduce. Production capacity, aircraft and ground operations are logistical components of this broader strategy.","The strategy works alongside surveillance, reporting, veterinary response and animal-movement controls. No single intervention guarantees that an outbreak will disappear immediately. A public-domain USDA photo from June 2026 shows a sterile-fly ground release operation in Central Texas; it documents response activity but is not a photograph of the confirmed infected calf or dog."]},{"heading":"What livestock and pet owners should know","paragraphs":["Owners should check animals for wounds and unusual signs of infestation, particularly after an injury or travel from an affected area. Contact a veterinarian and the relevant animal-health authority promptly if a suspicious wound or larvae are found. Official guidance is the appropriate source for collection, submission, treatment and any reporting requirements; those rules may change during an active outbreak.","Because the pest can affect pets and wildlife as well as cattle, landowners and animal shelters should also know where to find current USDA and Texas alerts. A livestock shipment, rescue animal or traveling companion animal may raise different regulatory questions. This report describes the historical emergence and public response, not individualized veterinary care or interstate transport clearance."]},{"heading":"Separating July reporting from later status","paragraphs":["The initial USDA confirmations date to early June 2026, and the first Keep TX Red story in this series had been published by July 1. Subsequent official updates reported additional cases and changes to affected areas. For a live tally, Texas Animal Health Commission and APHIS status dashboards should take precedence over numbers quoted in an earlier article.","News coverage should distinguish lab-confirmed detections from suspect cases, geographic regions under surveillance from places with confirmed infections, and a successful sterile-fly release from proof the parasite has been eradicated. Keeping those distinctions clear helps owners and veterinarians respond without either minimizing a serious animal-health threat or overstating an unsupported claim."]},{"heading":"Restoration record and image disclosure","paragraphs":["The original editorial text at this July 1 Keep TX Red URL no longer survives in the database or repository. This version was reconstructed October 14, 2026 from the primary June USDA announcements, Texas agency guidance and the January sterile-fly dispersal notice. It does not quote vanished copy or claim that all field operations shown in the cited records occurred at the same time.","The proposed photograph is a USDA public-domain image of a New World screwworm sterile-fly ground release chamber operation in Central Texas in June 2026. It is offered only for the site''s existing governed visual-match audit; it is not presented as an image of an infected calf, a specific property with confirmed detections, or the July news event before the visual review approves it."]}],"faq":[{"q":"When was New World screwworm first confirmed in Texas in 2026?","a":"USDA announced a laboratory-confirmed detection in a Zavala County calf on June 3, 2026, and published additional confirmations in early June."},{"q":"Can New World screwworm affect people?","a":"Yes, though USDA identifies livestock, pets and wildlife as major hosts and human infestation as less common. Seek appropriate medical care for any suspected human wound infestation."},{"q":"Where are current Texas affected counties listed?","a":"The Texas Animal Health Commission and USDA APHIS provide current dashboards and movement guidance; those are more appropriate for live decisions than historical news."}],"sources":[{"label":"USDA APHIS — first confirmed Texas detection June 3, 2026","url":"https://www.aphis.usda.gov/news/agency-announcements/usda-confirms-presence-new-world-screwworm-united-states"},{"label":"USDA APHIS — June 8 additional detections","url":"https://www.aphis.usda.gov/news/agency-announcements/usda-confirms-two-additional-cases-new-world-screwworm-united-states"},{"label":"USDA APHIS — June 9 coordinated response","url":"https://direct.aphis.usda.gov/news/agency-announcements/usda-continues-lead-coordinated-response-new-world-screwworm-new-case"},{"label":"USDA APHIS — January 30 sterile-fly dispersal strategy","url":"https://direct.aphis.usda.gov/news/agency-announcements/usda-shifts-sterile-fly-dispersal-efforts-defend-us-border"},{"label":"Texas Animal Health Commission — live screwworm outbreak guidance","url":"https://www.tahc.texas.gov/emergency/nws.html"},{"label":"USDA Commons photograph — sterile fly ground release, public domain","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:New_World_Screwworm_Response_in_Central_Texas_--_Sterile_Fly_Ground_Release_Chamber_Operations_(55351666851).jpg"}],"keyTakeaways":["USDA confirmed New World screwworm in a Texas calf on June 3, 2026 and documented more animal cases that month.","State and federal response included surveillance, investigation and sterile-fly dispersal, not just border warnings.","Current movement rules and county counts require the live USDA and Texas agency dashboards, not a July article."]}'::jsonb,
